WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.900
A pellet named mitochondria is never only mitochondria.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.680
So how do you find out what is really in it?

3
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:17.260
In 1955 a group in Louvain broke rat liver gently, so the organelles stayed mostly

4
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.820
whole, and spun it into five fractions.

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.620
They measured enzymes in every fraction.

6
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.880
The mitochondrial marker peaked in one place.

7
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.980
Acid phosphatase did not travel with it.

8
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:32.373
Here is a made-up table for rat liver.

9
00:00:33.093 --> 00:00:35.493
Each row adds up to 100 percent.

10
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:40.953
Take the percent of an enzyme found in a fraction, and divide by the percent of the

11
00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:42.053
protein found there.

12
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:44.933
This is the relative specific activity.

13
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:51.453
Fraction L holds 45 percent of the acid phosphatase in 5 percent of the protein.

14
00:00:52.073 --> 00:00:56.773
45 over 5 is 9. The fraction is enriched 9-fold.

15
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:00.267
Now do the same for every fraction.

16
00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:08.027
Cytochrome oxidase, the mitochondrial marker, peaks in fraction M. Acid phosphatase,

17
00:01:08.387 --> 00:01:15.707
the lysosome marker, peaks in fraction L. An unknown enzyme, called X, follows acid

18
00:01:15.707 --> 00:01:17.887
phosphatase, fraction by fraction.

19
00:01:18.547 --> 00:01:20.827
It does not follow cytochrome oxidase.

20
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.440
But enriched does not mean pure.

21
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.720
Fraction L also carries 15% of the cell's cytochrome oxidase.

22
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.640
For mitochondria, that is a relative specific activity of 3 in the same fraction.

23
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.400
More than half the acid phosphatase is elsewhere.

24
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.060
So L is enriched in lysosomes, but it is not pure.

25
00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.380
That is why several markers are read together.

26
00:01:49.233 --> 00:01:55.193
Next question. Is X inside a particle, or stuck to the outside of it?

27
00:01:56.293 --> 00:01:57.913
Latency is a test for this.

28
00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:02.433
It is activity that cannot be measured until a membrane is opened.

29
00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:08.673
Suppose only 10% of the activity of X can be measured in intact particles.

30
00:02:09.373 --> 00:02:16.153
Add detergent, and it rises to 100%. The substrate cannot reach an enzyme sealed

31
00:02:16.153 --> 00:02:17.213
behind a membrane.

32
00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:21.153
So X is 90% latent, and sits inside.

33
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:25.127
So X is provisionally lysosomal.

34
00:02:25.807 --> 00:02:30.787
To confirm it, you want a second lysosomal marker that tracks X on a density

35
00:02:30.787 --> 00:02:34.767
gradient, and images of the protein in intact cells.

36
00:02:35.727 --> 00:02:39.987
A shared peak alone can come from two organelles that sediment together.

37
00:02:39.987 --> 00:02:45.347
Read the whole distribution, and never take a pellet's name for its contents.
